WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.378
File handling deserves special care because file names, paths, content type claims, archives,

00:00:05.378 --> 00:00:09.845
and metadata can all be influenced by users or external systems.

00:00:09.845 --> 00:00:14.311
A file upload feature should treat the uploaded file as untrusted.

00:00:14.311 --> 00:00:19.183
The application should control storage location, generated names, size limits, allowed types,

00:00:19.183 --> 00:00:20.807
scanning or review requirements,

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:24.868
and access permissions rather than trusting client-provided names or headers.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:29.740
Path traversal occurs when input influences file access outside an intended directory.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:35.018
The defensive idea is to avoid using raw user input as a path,

00:00:35.018 --> 00:00:38.672
normalize and constrain paths carefully, use allowlists where possible,

00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:41.921
and separate public files from private internal storage.

00:00:41.921 --> 00:00:48.417
Temporary files should have predictable lifecycle management so sensitive data does not remain longer than intended.

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:49.067
Node.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:53.533
js can launch child processes, but unsafe command construction is dangerous.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:57.187
User-controlled input should not be concatenated into shell commands.

00:00:57.187 --> 00:01:00.436
Prefer APIs that pass arguments without shell interpretation,

00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:04.902
restrict what operations are allowed, and apply operating system permissions, timeouts,

00:01:04.902 --> 00:01:06.120
and resource limits.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.992
The application should not rely on one validation check as the only

00:01:10.992 --> 00:01:13.022
barrier before a system-level effect.

00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:15.053
Server-side request forgery, or SSRF,

00:01:15.053 --> 00:01:20.737
is a risk when server-side URL fetching can be influenced to access unintended resources.

00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:26.421
The service may be able to reach internal networks, cloud metadata endpoints, admin panels,

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:29.263
or services that users cannot reach directly.

00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:32.918
Safe outbound request controls include allowlists, URL parsing discipline,

00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:37.140
network egress restrictions, timeouts, response limits, and careful handling of redirects.
